# KaminIDE patches — gpui-pre-windows 0.3.8 (zed 279fe070bb389b79652e52065b2f001edcc0b11b)
#
# 1. Font-size-adjust: each fallback face that a normalized run selects is pinned before shaping as its own format range at its raw-metric used size, with CSS-run boundaries and native vertical design advances.
# 2. Effect lifetime: complete scenes, including kept frames, retain only their needed scratch and immutable mask uploads; embedded captures have independent scratch. Mask uploads are reported as GPU owners and scenes with more than 64 masks reuse their uploads without a count cliff.
# 3. Frame canvas: the frame is drawn into a kept canvas texture and each back buffer receives its accumulated damage by copy before present; a scroll step planned as a move (`Scene::frame_shift`) copies the canvas by the step through a scratch texture and rasterizes only the planned rectangles, from instances uploaded once; capture reads the canvas and reports the plan, rectangles and GPU wait.
# 4. Analytic gradients: D3D and WARP evaluate radial, repeating and arbitrary-stop sRGB fills directly in the destination, with retained descriptor/stop buffers, shared group uploads and explicit memory accounting; no intermediate raster tile. Analytic quads draw as order-preserving runs with a pixel program of their own, so the plain quad and path programs carry neither the stop loop nor its buffers.
# 5. Atlas lifetime: keep only live texture slots with monotonic checked ids; reclaim empty pages without tombstones or stale-scene id reuse.
# 6. Debug shaders: embed both HLSL sources and their shared include, compile with rooted virtual filenames and the existing flags, and keep dev/agent binaries independent of the build worktree. The scoped include callback borrows static bytes until synchronous D3DCompile returns; its raw out-pointers are supplied by the compiler. Release keeps its existing embedded bytecode.
# 7. Frame demand: wake idle Windows windows asynchronously through the existing GPUI invalidator, coalescing WM_PAINT and retaining paced vsync retry during native draws; weak window ownership prevents stale HWND requests.
# 8. Settled subtree skipping: ink sinks collect the placed bounds of every primitive a scope offers (culled included, deferred paint snapshots feed the sinks open at capture) and are tainted by input, element state, groups or callbacks unless a scope declares them inert while unseen; exact layout leaves stand in for inflexible column items whose descendant margins do not adjoin their edges, and are plain leaves of the retained frame tree.
# 9. Frame diagnostics: opt-in host logging exposes native draw force and pending force without changing requests.
# 10. Deferred frames: consume the pending forced request even when the incoming draw is already forced, preventing a stale extra presentation.
# 11. DirectX groups: one frame-domain staging surface preserves GPU interpolation exactly; scissored writes, dirty-rectangle clears, bounded pooled retained surfaces with blur margins, and rectangle-only blend backdrop copies.
# 12. Adapter policy: instance-owned typed DXGI preference, unchanged default enumeration, device recovery and live adapter LUID/driver identity.
# 13. DirectWrite: CSS font selection, shaping, vertical glyphs and fallback metrics.
# 14. Zero em size: a run at font-size 0 is shaped blank (no glyph, no advance, zero extent); a line of such runs is not shaped, a mixed line shapes them at a stand-in size no neighbour uses and drops their glyph runs.
# 15. DirectX: CSS primitives, images, filters, masks, gradients and per-window frame readback with copy/map duration delivered to the explicit host endpoint.
# 16. Window lifecycle: per-window rendering/resize policy and deterministic WPT scale.
# 17. Process shutdown: drain deferred native window destruction for at most 5 s (logged if a window is never released), stop and join owned vsync threads while pumping messages, then release GPU/text devices before leaving the platform loop. A window revokes its OLE drop target in WM_DESTROY, while the HWND is alive, so a window destroyed by the system releases it.
# 18. Diagnostics: application-owned GPU batch measurements and typed startup configuration survive device recovery; environment parsing belongs to the host.
# 19. Tests: initialize unfragmented backdrop fixtures against the paired GPUI surface fields.
# 20. Unsafe invariants: added unsafe blocks are windows-rs COM/Win32 calls on interfaces owned by the live DirectX/DirectWrite state, called on the thread that owns that state, with HRESULTs and out-parameters checked before use. Raw memory outside embedded debug shader compilation is touched in three places only: the frame-capture readback copies w*4 bytes per row of a w x h BGRA8 staging texture between Map and Unmap (RowPitch >= w*4), GPU timing reads a query result through the raw GetData vtable entry into a plain-data out-value whose size is passed with it, and external-texture registration adopts a non-null ID3D11Texture2D pointer already AddRef'd by the caller (from_raw takes that reference). Release builds never select the debug-only WARP/D3D11On12 device overrides.
# 21. Offscreen windows: a platform option creates message-only windows whose renderer draws into a render-target texture of the same device, format and shaders instead of a swap chain, with no DirectComposition target, Present, DWM attributes or resize presentation boost; coalesced posted paint messages replace WM_PAINT for frame demand and vsync pacing, and device recovery rebuilds the offscreen target. Measurement and pixel-gate hosts read frames back without loading the desktop compositor.
# 22. Presentation never blocks the UI thread (BR-31A): Present uses DXGI_PRESENT_DO_NOT_WAIT; a frame dropped by it or by the frame-latency gate keeps the gate count it took and makes the next paint present the current scene; Present time and dropped frames feed the frame counters.
# 23. GPU memory report: the DirectX renderer names every texture it holds (atlas pages with their allocated share, frame and path targets, group and blur buffers) by owner path, on request only; the software rasterizer is an explicit adapter preference that no selector string parses to, so optimized benchmark builds can measure software rendering while application selection is unchanged.
# 24. Damage redraw: the target keeps the last frame and every buffer (offscreen texture or flip-model back buffer) carries the damage accumulated since it was drawn; a frame is drawn only within that rectangle under a scissor, groups whose composite cannot reach it are skipped, and an unchanged frame is neither drawn nor presented. A different size, background, opaque backdrop, atlas epoch (removed tiles, replaced or forgotten external textures, device loss), a sampled external texture, a failed pass or GPUI_DAMAGE_REDRAW=0 draw the whole frame. Frame capture keeps one staging texture per size. Group images stay in the group atlas between frames: a group matched with a group of the last frame (Scene::group_base) keeps that group's atlas rectangle and image and is not drawn again; the other groups take free space on the shelves of the last full layout, and the atlas is packed again when they do not fit.
# 25. Retained group pixel auditing is an explicit GPUI_GROUP_SCRUB=1 diagnostic; normal frames validate content and resource identity without staging readbacks or audit redraws. Only a busy GPU defers an audit readback; device failures propagate to renderer recovery.
# 26. Unknown cursor: when GetCursorPos fails (a desktop that takes no input, a locked session) the pointer is reported outside the client area, not at the screen origin, so it hovers no hitbox.
# 27. Path batches: a batch clears and rasterizes its 4x multisampled intermediate only within the whole pixels its paths' clipped bounds reach inside the target and the bound scissor, draws nothing when that area is empty, and its sprites average the samples in integers themselves, so the intermediate has no resolved copy and no whole-window clear or resolve.
# 28. Transformed instances in partial redraws: a frame drawn within rectangles draws an instance a matrix moves once, with its own content mask, under each rectangle's scissor; cutting its mask to a rectangle would clip its triangles anew (SV_ClipDistance, clipped as geometry on WARP) and move its edge pixels away from the whole frame's.
#
# Generated by vendor/regenerate-patch.py; see docs/VENDORING.md.
